@kohlrabicode Great work! 

I think that using an approach more like we use for empty vertex arrays might be more suitable thought (e.g. with `ARRAY_FLAG_USES_EMPTY_VERTEX_ARRAY`). That way you can set the flag at mesh creation time. Most of the cases where this warning has caught assets (and the reason it was created) was that people were often creating / importing assets without tangents and then throwing a normal mapped material on to them (usually the StandardMaterial3D) and having visual errors that they couldn't track down. 

After we added the warning, we added code that forces the creation of tangents when importing meshes, so we no longer have the issue with imported meshes. It is just with manually created meshes. Therefore, I think it makes the most sense to have the workaround be at mesh creation time so you can flag to the renderer that you will be creating your own tangents in the shader. Importantly, this allows users to continue using the StandardMaterial3D if they want. And means that the mesh can be used with any material without triggering the error.

@clayjohn thank you for the feedback! I can certainly implement a flag for the ArrayMesh, but that won't fix the issue for other mesh sources that may not have tangents I believe. Logically, the tangent generation in shader should be a property of the shader, not the mesh that's being used. Theoretically, you could use the same mesh with a shader that generates tangents and with one that doesn't where the warning would be justified.

Am I missing something here? Is this somehow related to the next pass where you use a StandardMaterial3D in the next pass after generating tangents in the first pass?

In the mean time, I'll get busy prototyping the approach of adding a flag to the ArrayMesh, thank you for the speedy response!
